#!/bin/sh
# Blocks commits containing banned patterns in staged files.
# This repo is PUBLIC — see CLAUDE.md "never commit environment details".
#
# Enable in a fresh clone (git does not install hooks automatically):
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# THIS IS A NARROW GUARD. Both limits verified by black-box probe:
#
#   1. Everything is scanned except the built root index.html,
#      package-lock.json, and the scanner itself. docs/, tests/, the
#      hand-written setup.html and the src/index.html template ARE scanned.
#      The one carve-out is a short RESERVED list in scripts/check-secrets.cjs:
#      13 exact literals allowed under tests/ ONLY,
#      because they are the subject of the tests asserting on them (isValidLanIp)
#      rather than real addresses. docs/ gets NO exemption — placeholders are the
#      only thing that passes there. Whole-match comparison, so a neighbouring
#      value one digit off still fails, and in src/ the reserved literals fail
#      like any other. smoke-build.cjs step 15 stops this carve-out widening.
#   2. It matches SHAPES: RFC1918 and CGNAT/Tailscale IPv4, IPv6 unique-local
#      host addresses, .local/.lan/.home.arpa/.internal hostnames, user-home
#      paths, private-key blocks, some API-token prefixes, non-zero lat:/lng:.
#      NOT caught: 127.0.0.1 and link-local (deliberately), any other hostname,
#      bare usernames, other absolute paths (D:\..., /root/), SSH key paths,
#      and secrets with no recognisable prefix.
#
# It scans the STAGED blob of each file, not the working copy, so a secret that
# is staged and then edited out of the file without re-staging is still caught.
#
# A staged file the scanner cannot READ is a hard failure, named in the output,
# not a silent skip: a run that opens nothing can no longer print the success
# line. Staged deletions are excluded before that check, so a plain `git rm`
# does not trip it.
#
# So a green hook is not proof a commit is clean. It catches the one mistake
# that has actually happened here before; the rest is still on the author.
#
# CI runs the same scan (the `secrets` job in .github/workflows/build.yml). That
# job stages the diff under review first — a bare `npm run check:secrets` step
# would find nothing staged, report "checked 0 staged files" and pass
# unconditionally.
#
# But CI sees only the NET base..HEAD diff. A secret added in one commit and
# removed in a later one stages as nothing and scans clean — while still sitting
# in the pushed branch's history, which on a public repo stays fetchable via
# refs/pull/N/head even after a squash merge. Stopping that is this hook's job,
# not CI's.
#
# CI does NOT rescue limits 1 and 2: it runs the same scanner, so it inherits the
# reserved-fixture carve-out and the shape-only patterns.
npm run check:secrets || exit 1
